    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d691">

                <pb facs="0664" n="660" xml:id="pg660"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.002/507: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-02-23T17:00:00-05:00">February 23, 1938—5
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>159. Your 252, February 12, 8 p.m. The Commissioner’s letter of October 22 would
                    seem to afford a superficial basis for an assertion that the failure of the
                    Tientsin Customs House to remit its quota for the servicing of foreign
                    obligations secured on the Customs revenue was the result of the Commissioner’s
                    own decision.</p>
                <p>Please confer with your British colleague and, having in mind the manner in which
                    the text of the letter became known, telegraph the Department whether
                    information is available which would make the record reflect the true situation
                    in regard to remittances. All reports received indicate to the Department that
                    the letter represents part of an arrangement forced upon the Commissioner by the
                    Japanese.</p>
                <p>Please repeat this telegram and your reply to Hankow and Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
